        "problem": "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x}{4x+{x}^{2}}=$\n{{field-1}}",
        "worked_solution": "The numerator here is a bounded, oscillating function with \n\n$$\n|-2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x|\\le 2.\n$$\n\nHowever, the denominator $4x+{x}^{2}$ grows without bound as $x\\to \\infty .$ Since the denominator grows much faster than the (bounded) numerator, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x}{4x+{x}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nWe can see this from the graph of $y=\\frac{-2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x}{4x+{x}^{2}},$ shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13951126/assets/dd869f9140a12c19a0672963c064a4581767a3e39fac8c49b76b6e106755080f.png)",
